    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# 4.00 — Manifolds: A Recap\n\n**This notebook is a bridge, not a development.** It fixes notation and reminds you of four ideas that Stage 4 uses constantly. The full construction — with proofs, pathologies and the point-set care they need — is in [topology-journey Stage 3](https://github.com/cmsd2/topology-journey), and this notebook cross-references it rather than repeating it.\n\n| Idea | Full treatment |\n|---|---|\n| charts, atlases, topological manifolds | topology-journey **3.01** |\n| smooth structures, smooth maps | topology-journey **3.02** |\n| tangent vectors as derivations, $TM$, $T^*M$ | topology-journey **3.03** |\n| differential forms, wedge, $d$ | topology-journey **3.04** |\n\nIf those are fresh, skim this and move to 4.01.\n\n## Why Stage 4 needs them\n\nStage 3 did all its geometry on surfaces **sitting in $\\mathbb R^3$**. Every construction leaned on the ambient space: the normal $n$, the second fundamental form, the Gauss map. Then 3.06 proved the Theorema Egregium — $K$ is computable from $E, F, G$ alone — and the ambient space turned out to be scaffolding for the one quantity that mattered most.\n\nRiemann's move, in his 1854 lecture, is to **remove the scaffolding entirely**. Specify a space and a metric on it; ask for no surrounding space at all. To do that you need somewhere for the metric to live, and that is what a manifold provides:\n\n$$\\text{manifold} = \\text{a space that looks locally like } \\mathbb R^n, \\text{ consistently.}$$\n\nTwo payoffs justify the abstraction:\n\n- **Dimension stops mattering.** Surfaces were $n = 2$ because we could only embed so much in $\\mathbb R^3$. Manifolds work in any dimension, which is required for the 4-dimensional spacetime of 6.02.\n- **Spaces with no natural embedding become available.** The hyperbolic plane, which by Hilbert's theorem (3.05) does *not* embed completely in $\\mathbb R^3$, is a perfectly ordinary Riemannian manifold. So is $SO(3)$, and so is the space of all lines through the origin.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 1. Charts, and why more than one is needed\n\nA **chart** is a homeomorphism $\\varphi : U \\to \\tilde U \\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ from an open piece of the space to an open piece of $\\mathbb R^n$ — a coordinate system valid on part of the manifold. An **atlas** is a collection of charts covering everything, and the manifold is **smooth** when all transition maps\n\n$$\\varphi_\\beta \\circ \\varphi_\\alpha^{-1}$$\n\nare smooth where defined. That compatibility condition is the whole definition: the charts must agree about what \"smooth\" means on their overlap.\n\n**One chart is rarely enough**, and the sphere is the standard example: no single chart covers $S^2$, because $S^2$ is compact and $\\mathbb R^2$ is not. Spherical coordinates fail at the poles — 3.07 met this as the geodesic equation's $\\cot u$ blowing up at $u = 0$ and $u = \\pi$, which is a defect of the chart and not of the sphere.\n\nTwo **stereographic** charts do cover it. Projecting from the north pole gives coordinates on $S^2\\setminus\\{N\\}$, from the south pole on $S^2\\setminus\\{S\\}$, and together they miss nothing. We met the north-pole projection in 2.05, where it identified $S^2$ with $\\hat{\\mathbb C}$; here it is doing a different job, as one chart of an atlas.\n\nThe transition map between them turns out to be inversion, $z \\mapsto 1/\\bar z$ in complex notation — smooth on the overlap $S^2 \\setminus \\{N, S\\}$, which is exactly what the smooth-structure condition demands. Let us check it.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nload(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\n\n/* Two stereographic charts on the unit sphere X² + Y² + Z² = 1.\n   From the north pole N = (0,0,1), and from the south pole S = (0,0,−1). */\nphiN(X_,Y_,Z_) := [ X_/(1 - Z_), Y_/(1 - Z_) ]$      /* undefined at N */\nphiS(X_,Y_,Z_) := [ X_/(1 + Z_), Y_/(1 + Z_) ]$      /* undefined at S */\n\n/* Their inverses, back onto the sphere */\ninvN(a_,b_) := block([d], d : a_^2 + b_^2 + 1,\n  [ 2*a_/d, 2*b_/d, (a_^2 + b_^2 - 1)/d ])$\n\nprint(\"check: φ_N ∘ φ_N⁻¹ = identity\")$\npt : invN(a, b)$\nprint(\"   φ_N⁻¹(a,b) on the sphere? |p|² = \",\n      ratsimp( pt[1]^2 + pt[2]^2 + pt[3]^2 ))$\nprint(\"   φ_N(φ_N⁻¹(a,b)) = \", ratsimp( phiN(pt[1], pt[2], pt[3]) ))$\n\n/* The transition map φ_S ∘ φ_N⁻¹ */\ntrans : ratsimp( phiS(pt[1], pt[2], pt[3]) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"transition map φ_S ∘ φ_N⁻¹ (a,b) = \", trans)$\nprint(\"   i.e. (a,b) ↦ (a, b)/(a² + b²) — inversion in the unit circle\")$\nprint(\"   (2.05 met this as z ↦ 1/z̄ )\")$\n\n/* It is smooth wherever it is defined: the only bad point is a = b = 0,\n   which is the image of the SOUTH pole, excluded from the overlap. */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"its Jacobian:\")$\nJ : matrix([diff(trans[1],a), diff(trans[1],b)],\n           [diff(trans[2],a), diff(trans[2],b)])$\nprint(\"   J = \", ratsimp(J))$\nprint(\"   det J = \", ratsimp(determinant(J)))$\nprint(\"   nonzero except at a = b = 0 — the south pole, not in the overlap.\")$\nprint(\"   So the two charts are smoothly compatible, and S² is a smooth manifold.\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 1,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "check: φ_N ∘ φ_N⁻¹ = identity\n   φ_N⁻¹(a,b) on the sphere? |p|² =  1\n   φ_N(φ_N⁻¹(a,b)) =  [a,b]\ntransition map φ_S ∘ φ_N⁻¹ (a,b) =  [a/(b^2+a^2),b/(b^2+a^2)]\n   i.e. (a,b) ↦ (a, b)/(a² + b²) — inversion in the unit circle\n   (2.05 met this as z ↦ 1/z̄ )\nits Jacobian:\n   J =\n       matrix([(b^2-a^2)/(b^4+2*a^2*b^2+a^4),-((2*a*b)/(b^4+2*a^2*b^2+a^4))],\n              [-((2*a*b)/(b^4+2*a^2*b^2+a^4)),\n               -((b^2-a^2)/(b^4+2*a^2*b^2+a^4))])\n   det J =  -(1/(b^4+2*a^2*b^2+a^4))\n   nonzero except at a = b = 0 — the south pole, not in the overlap.\n   So the two charts are smoothly compatible, and S² is a smooth manifold."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "*(Aside: $\\det J < 0$, so the two standard stereographic charts induce **opposite** orientations. To orient $S^2$ one composes one of them with a reflection, e.g. $(a,b)\\mapsto(a,-b)$. Orientability is exactly the question of whether such a consistent choice exists across a whole atlas — it does for $S^2$, and does not for $\\mathbb{RP}^2$, as 2.02 noted.)*\n\n## 2. Tangent vectors, without arrows\n\nOn a surface in $\\mathbb R^3$ a tangent vector was an arrow in the ambient space, lying in the tangent plane. With the ambient space gone, that definition is unavailable — and the replacement is the one genuinely unintuitive step in the whole subject.\n\n**A tangent vector at $p$ is a derivation**: a linear map $v$ on smooth functions near $p$ satisfying the Leibniz rule\n\n$$v(fg) = v(f)\\,g(p) + f(p)\\,v(g).$$\n\nThe motivation: an arrow's only geometric job is to differentiate functions along itself. Directional derivative $\\mapsto$ arrow is a bijection in $\\mathbb R^n$, so we keep the derivative and discard the arrow. Everything an arrow could do, a derivation does.\n\n**The coordinate basis.** In a chart $(x^1,\\dots,x^n)$ the partial derivatives\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x^1}\\bigg|_p,\\;\\dots,\\;\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x^n}\\bigg|_p$$\n\nform a basis of $T_pM$, so $\\dim T_pM = n$ and a tangent vector is $v = v^i\\,\\partial_i$ as usual. What is *not* as usual: the components $v^i$ depend on the chart, and under a change of coordinates they transform by the Jacobian,\n\n$$\\tilde v^j = \\frac{\\partial \\tilde x^j}{\\partial x^i}\\,v^i .$$\n\nThis is the origin of the index gymnastics of Stage 4. A \"vector\" is not a list of numbers; it is a list of numbers *together with a rule for how they change when the chart changes*. Objects transforming that way are called **contravariant**; those transforming by the inverse Jacobian — like the components of $df$ — are **covariant**, and live in the cotangent space $T_p^*M$.\n\nThe metric $g_{ij}$ of 4.01 will be doubly covariant, which is why the line element $g_{ij}dx^idx^j$ comes out chart-independent even though every symbol in it is chart-dependent.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* --- Derivations satisfy Leibniz --- */\ndepends([ff, gg], [x, y])$\nv(h) := aa*diff(h, x) + bb*diff(h, y)$        /* a general tangent vector at a point */\n\nprint(\"Leibniz rule for v = a ∂/∂x + b ∂/∂y:\")$\nprint(\"   v(fg) − [v(f)·g + f·v(g)] = \", ratsimp( v(ff*gg) - (v(ff)*gg + ff*v(gg)) ))$\nprint(\"   ✓ — so v is a derivation, and that is all a tangent vector is\")$\n\n/* Linearity too */\nprint(\"   v(αf + βg) − [αv(f) + βv(g)] = \",\n      ratsimp( v(al*ff + be*gg) - (al*v(ff) + be*v(gg)) ))$\n\n/* --- Components transform by the Jacobian --- */\n/* Polar coordinates as a second chart on (part of) the plane. */\nremove([ff,gg], dependency)$\nxofr : r*cos(th)$   yofr : r*sin(th)$\nJac : matrix([diff(xofr,r), diff(xofr,th)],\n             [diff(yofr,r), diff(yofr,th)])$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"chart change (r,θ) → (x,y):  ∂(x,y)/∂(r,θ) = \", Jac)$\nprint(\"   det = \", trigsimp(determinant(Jac)), \"  — the familiar polar area factor\")$\n\n/* Take the vector field ∂/∂θ and express it in Cartesian components. */\n/* ∂/∂θ = (∂x/∂θ) ∂/∂x + (∂y/∂θ) ∂/∂y */\ndth_x : diff(xofr, th)$  dth_y : diff(yofr, th)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"the field ∂/∂θ in Cartesian components:\")$\nprint(\"   ∂/∂θ = \", dth_x, \" ∂/∂x + \", dth_y, \" ∂/∂y\")$\nprint(\"        = −y ∂/∂x + x ∂/∂y   (substituting x = r cos θ, y = r sin θ)\")$\nprint(\"   — the rotation field, which 1.03 met as the generator of SO(2)\")$\n\n/* Verify by applying both forms to a test function */\nf_test : x^2 + 3*y$\nlhs_ : ratsimp( diff( subst([x = xofr, y = yofr], f_test), th ) )$\nrhs_ : ratsimp( subst([x = xofr, y = yofr], -y*diff(f_test,x) + x*diff(f_test,y)) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"acting on f = x² + 3y:\")$\nprint(\"   ∂/∂θ in polar   : \", lhs_)$\nprint(\"   (−y∂ₓ + x∂_y) f : \", rhs_)$\nprint(\"   difference      : \", trigsimp(lhs_ - rhs_), \"  ✓ same derivation, two charts\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 2,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "Leibniz rule for v = a ∂/∂x + b ∂/∂y:\n   v(fg) − [v(f)·g + f·v(g)] =  0\n   ✓ — so v is a derivation, and that is all a tangent vector is\n   v(αf + βg) − [αv(f) + βv(g)] =  0\nchart change (r,θ) → (x,y):  ∂(x,y)/∂(r,θ) =\n  matrix([cos(th),-(r*sin(th))],[sin(th),r*cos(th)])\n   det =  r   — the familiar polar area factor\nthe field ∂/∂θ in Cartesian components:\n   ∂/∂θ =  -(r*sin(th))  ∂/∂x +  r*cos(th)  ∂/∂y\n        = −y ∂/∂x + x ∂/∂y   (substituting x = r cos θ, y = r sin θ)\n   — the rotation field, which 1.03 met as the generator of SO(2)\nacting on f = x² + 3y:\n   ∂/∂θ in polar   :  3*r*cos(th)-2*r^2*cos(th)*sin(th)\n   (−y∂ₓ + x∂_y) f :  3*r*cos(th)-2*r^2*cos(th)*sin(th)\n   difference      :  0   ✓ same derivation, two charts"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 3. Covectors and forms, in one page\n\nDual to $T_pM$ is the **cotangent space** $T_p^*M$, spanned by $dx^1,\\dots,dx^n$ with $dx^i(\\partial_j) = \\delta^i_j$. The differential of a function,\n\n$$df = \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x^i}\\,dx^i,$$\n\nis the model covector: it eats a tangent vector and returns the derivative of $f$ along it, $df(v) = v(f)$.\n\n**Forms** are the alternating multilinear generalisation. A $k$-form is built with the wedge product, which is associative, bilinear and **anticommuting**:\n\n$$dx \\wedge dy = -\\,dy\\wedge dx, \\qquad dx\\wedge dx = 0 .$$\n\nThis is the honest version of the cross product from 1.01 — as warned there, what generalises is $\\Lambda^k$, and in $\\mathbb R^3$ the coincidence $\\binom32 = 3$ let a 2-form masquerade as a vector.\n\nThe **exterior derivative** $d$ takes $k$-forms to $(k{+}1)$-forms, agrees with $df$ on functions, and satisfies\n\n$$d^2 = 0,$$\n\nwhich is just equality of mixed partials in disguise. Stokes' theorem, $\\int_M d\\omega = \\int_{\\partial M}\\omega$, unifies the classical Green/Gauss/Stokes theorems, and de Rham cohomology measures the failure of closed forms ($d\\omega = 0$) to be exact ($\\omega = d\\eta$).\n\n**All of this is developed properly in [topology-journey 3.04–3.06](https://github.com/cmsd2/topology-journey).** Stage 4 uses forms lightly — the volume form $\\sqrt{\\det g}\\,dx^1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge dx^n$, and the curvature 2-form in 5.05 — so the summary above plus the cross-reference is deliberately all that is offered here.\n\nOne check is worth doing, because $d^2 = 0$ is the engine of everything downstream.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* --- d² = 0 is equality of mixed partials --- */\n/* Represent a 1-form ω = P dx + Q dy by its coefficient list [P, Q].\n   Then dω = (∂Q/∂x − ∂P/∂y) dx∧dy, a single coefficient. */\ndepends([P, Q, F], [x, y])$\n\nd0(f_)     := [ diff(f_, x), diff(f_, y) ]$          /* function → 1-form  */\nd1(om)     := diff(om[2], x) - diff(om[1], y)$        /* 1-form  → 2-form  */\n\nprint(\"for a function F:\")$\nprint(\"   dF        = \", d0(F), \"  (as coefficients of dx, dy)\")$\nprint(\"   d(dF)     = \", ratsimp( d1(d0(F)) ))$\nprint(\"   — zero because ∂²F/∂x∂y = ∂²F/∂y∂x.  That is all d² = 0 says.\")$\n\n/* A concrete closed-but-not-exact form: the angle form on the punctured plane */\nom_theta : [ -y/(x^2+y^2), x/(x^2+y^2) ]$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"the angle form ω = (−y dx + x dy)/(x² + y²):\")$\nprint(\"   dω = \", ratsimp( diff(om_theta[2],x) - diff(om_theta[1],y) ),\n      \"   — closed\")$\nprint(\"   but ∮ω around the unit circle = \",\n      integrate( ratsimp( subst([x = cos(t), y = sin(t)],\n        om_theta[1]*(-sin(t)) + om_theta[2]*cos(t)) ), t, 0, 2*%pi ))$\nprint(\"   nonzero, so ω is NOT exact: no F on the punctured plane has dF = ω.\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Closed but not exact — the failure is measured by de Rham cohomology,\")$\nprint(\"and it detects the hole. This is the same information the Umlaufsatz\")$\nprint(\"(3.01) and Gauss–Bonnet (3.08) were reporting, in the language forms use.\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 3,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "for a function F:\n   dF        =  ['diff(F,x,1),'diff(F,y,1)]   (as coefficients of dx, dy)\n   d(dF)     =  0\n   — zero because ∂²F/∂x∂y = ∂²F/∂y∂x.  That is all d² = 0 says.\nthe angle form ω = (−y dx + x dy)/(x² + y²):\n   dω =  0    — closed\n   but ∮ω around the unit circle =  2*%pi\n   nonzero, so ω is NOT exact: no F on the punctured plane has dF = ω.\nClosed but not exact — the failure is measured by de Rham cohomology,\nand it detects the hole. This is the same information the Umlaufsatz\n(3.01) and Gauss–Bonnet (3.08) were reporting, in the language forms use."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Summary — the vocabulary Stage 4 assumes\n\n| Object | Is | Transforms |\n|---|---|---|\n| chart $\\varphi: U \\to \\mathbb R^n$ | local coordinates | — |\n| smooth structure | atlas with smooth transitions | — |\n| tangent vector $v \\in T_pM$ | a **derivation**: linear, Leibniz | by the Jacobian (contravariant) |\n| $\\partial_1,\\dots,\\partial_n$ | basis of $T_pM$ | — |\n| covector $\\in T_p^*M$ | linear functional on $T_pM$ | by the inverse Jacobian (covariant) |\n| $k$-form | alternating, built with $\\wedge$ | — |\n| $d$ | exterior derivative, $d^2 = 0$ | — |\n\nChecked here: the two stereographic charts on $S^2$ are smoothly compatible, with transition map the inversion $(a,b)\\mapsto(a,b)/(a^2+b^2)$ and $\\det J = -1/(a^2+b^2)^2$ — nonzero on the overlap, and negative, so the charts are oppositely oriented. A general $v = a\\partial_x + b\\partial_y$ satisfies Leibniz identically. The field $\\partial/\\partial\\theta$ equals $-y\\partial_x + x\\partial_y$, verified by applying both to a test function. And $d^2 = 0$ reduces to equality of mixed partials, while the angle form is closed with $\\oint\\omega = 2\\pi$ — closed but not exact, detecting the puncture.\n\n## The one thing to carry forward\n\n**A \"vector\" is components plus a transformation rule.** Nothing in Stage 4 is a bare list of numbers. $g_{ij}$, $\\Gamma^k_{ij}$, $R^l_{ijk}$ all carry indices whose position records how they respond to a change of chart, and the whole point of the formalism is that the *geometry* — lengths, angles, curvature — comes out chart-independent even though every component is chart-dependent.\n\nThe Christoffel symbols are the instructive exception: they carry indices but are **not** a tensor. They transform with an extra inhomogeneous term, which is precisely why they can be made to vanish at a point (normal coordinates, 3.07) — and why curvature, which *is* a tensor, cannot.\n\n## Next\n\n**4.01 — Riemannian Metrics** puts an inner product on each tangent space and lets it vary smoothly. Everything from 3.03 returns with the ambient space deleted: $E,F,G$ become $g_{ij}$, lengths and areas are computed from $g$ alone, and the sphere, the flat torus and the hyperbolic plane appear as metrics rather than as surfaces in space.\n\n## References\n\nThe full development of everything above:\n\n- ★ Lee, J.M. (2011) *Introduction to Topological Manifolds*. 2nd edn. New York: Springer. Chapters 2–4 for charts, atlases and the topological prerequisites.\n- ★ Lee, J.M. (2013) *Introduction to Smooth Manifolds*. 2nd edn. New York: Springer. The standard reference: Chapter 1 (smooth structures), Chapter 3 (tangent vectors as derivations), Chapter 11 (cotangent bundle), Chapter 14 (forms), Chapter 16 (Stokes).\n- [topology-journey](https://github.com/cmsd2/topology-journey) Stage 3 — 3.01 topological manifolds, 3.02 smooth structures, 3.03 tangent and cotangent bundles, 3.04 differential forms, 3.05 de Rham cohomology, 3.06 Stokes' theorem. **This is the companion treatment**; the present notebook exists only to fix notation.\n- ★ Naber, G.L. (1997) *Topology, Geometry, and Gauge Fields*. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-2742-5. Chapters 1–2, with the physics motivation running alongside.\n\nRiemann, B. (1854) *Über die Hypothesen, welche der Geometrie zu Grunde liegen* is the origin of the whole programme; the manifold concept took another seventy years to reach its modern form, largely through Weyl and Whitney.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = in the local indexed library (`maths` collection); the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell in this notebook was executed and its output is the real result; the prose is written from knowledge and checked against these sources where they cover the material.*",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}